[{"role": "system", "content": "You maintain `skill.md`, a short, general reference of design lessons for an automated process that's evolving a population of solitaire game variants (written in a custom format called SGDL) generation by generation.\n\nEach generation, many independently-running variants are each given exactly one focused revision based on their own simulated playtest results, by a separate process that has no visibility into what any of the other variants are doing. Whoever makes each revision also writes a short \"insight\": a general, transferable lesson suggested by that one specific change and its outcome, not a narration of what they did. You'll be given the current skill.md (or told it doesn't exist yet, if this is the first generation) plus the full batch of insights produced this generation, and your job is to produce the next version of skill.md.\n\nTreat skill.md as a living document you rewrite each time, not a log you append to:\n\n- Integrate new insights into the existing structure rather than tacking them on. If several insights from this generation (or an insight and an existing point) are really saying the same thing in different words, merge them into one clearer, possibly stronger statement -- don't keep near-duplicates as separate bullets.\n- If this generation's evidence contradicts or weakens an existing point, revise or remove it rather than letting both versions stand.\n- Look for what's well-supported. If most of this generation's relevant insights agree, that's worth keeping or strengthening; if only one or two insights suggest something that doesn't fit with everything else, it may just be noise from one particular variant rather than a real general lesson, and it's fine to leave out.\n- Write every point as a general principle about solitaire design, not as a fact about one specific file's exact numbers (\"foundation piles that no move rule ever targets sit permanently empty regardless of other changes\" is useful; \"Trizug's draw pile of 24 cards was too small\" is not, since nobody will recognize \"Trizug\" later).\n- Organize the document under a small number of clear headers (e.g. draw pile design, difficulty and winnability, avoiding unused elements, win conditions), and keep the whole thing short enough that it's actually useful as a quick reference -- a sprawling document nobody can read in full defeats the purpose. A specific cap on total bullet points is given to you below; treat it as a hard limit, not a target to approach.\n- It's completely fine for this to be a light touch-up most generations and a more substantial rewrite occasionally, depending on what the evidence actually supports.\n\nEnd your response with the complete new skill.md -- the whole document, not a diff -- in a single fenced code block tagged `skill`, as the last thing in your response. You can reason about what to keep, merge, or change beforehand.\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "There is no existing skill.md yet -- this is the first time it's being written, based only on the insights below.\n\nThis generation produced 100 insight(s) from independently revised variants:\n\n1. If the win condition requires tableau piles to empty, the rules need a genuine sink for cards; otherwise the game can only reshuffle congestion, not make progress. Also, an extra draw restriction that is almost never satisfiable can silently disable the core pacing mechanism, so draw gates should be used sparingly and only when they clearly support play.\n2. If a solitaire starts with zero legal moves across all shuffles, first fix structural accessibility before tuning difficulty: ensure there is at least one destination type that can accept opening moves, and make the win condition reachable from ordinary play. Also, repairing invalid or unsupported setup keywords is often more important than subtle rule balancing, because a syntactically \u201cclose\u201d design can still be functionally dead.\n3. When a solitaire variant stalls in every run while still generating long searches, the problem is often not \u201cdifficulty\u201d but a missing primitive action: players can see many states, but none of them lead to meaningful reduction. Adding a simple, broadly applicable move that lets cards consolidate or clear is often a better first fix than tuning setup counts or draw behavior.\n4. When a solitaire variant shows near-zero card usage and repeated search-cap failures, first check for structural deadness rather than \u201cdifficulty\u201d: inaccessible stock cards, invalid setup flags, or a win condition disconnected from actual card movement can make a game unplayable before balance even matters.\n\nA good first repair is to give every major component a real role in the win: the draw must expose cards, tableau moves must meaningfully reorganize them, and at least one destination pile type should accumulate long-term progress toward victory.\n5. If a pile type can receive cards but is not part of the win condition and has no way to release them, it tends to become either a pointless sink or something players avoid entirely. Giving such piles a reversible role and including them in victory usually improves both decision-making and overall component relevance.\n6. When a solitaire wins while using only a tiny fraction of its cards or piles, the first priority is usually not fine-tuning difficulty but reconnecting the win condition to the play space. A small rule change that makes existing tableau piles necessary for victory often improves meaningful decision-making more than adding new mechanics.\n7. When losses all hit the search cap with little variation, first suspect malformed or self-contradictory rules before tuning difficulty. In solitaire, a small set of internally consistent move rules usually improves both solvability and decision quality more than adding more exotic pile behaviors to a broken core.\n8. When a solitaire variant shows 0% card and pile usage across many exhausted losses, first suspect a rules-definition failure or an overconstrained move condition rather than \u201chigh difficulty.\u201d The best next step is usually a minimal repair to create a playable core loop before tuning challenge.\n9. When a solitaire variant already wins reasonably often but shows uneven card usage, tightening an overly permissive win condition can improve whether all piles and cards actually matter more effectively than immediately adding new move rules. Prefer fixing \u201cwhat counts as winning\u201d before expanding move complexity if the current successes may be bypassing parts of the design.\n10. If every tested deal is proven unwinnable in just a handful of moves with near-zero card usage, suspect a rules-connectivity failure before tuning difficulty: the piles exist, but the legal-move graph is disconnected. In early revisions, prefer broad, comprehensible base moves that guarantee some opening play; difficulty can be reintroduced once the game reliably engages its cards and piles.\n11. If every move rule names only one pile type, make sure there are at least two actual piles of that type unless self-moves are explicitly meaningful in the engine. A solitaire can look structurally valid on paper yet be completely unwinnable if its move graph has no real source-destination pairs at the initial state.\n12. If a solitaire layout has entire pile types that cannot receive from or send to the main flow of play, the game often fails not by being \u201chard\u201d but by being nonfunctional. First restore a connected move graph between pile types; only then tune difficulty with stricter build rules.\n13. When a solitaire variant is failing uniformly with search-cap losses, first check for structural incoherence rather than \u201cdifficulty\u201d: invalid or overly-permissive move rules often create huge, aimless state spaces without a workable path to finish. Tightening moves around a clear building/completion pattern usually improves both solvability and decision quality more than adding more freedom does.\n14. When a solitaire starts with zero legal moves in every run, the priority is not balance tuning but restoring a basic move network: at least one broadly available move from the initial layout, plus a win condition that depends on using the main pile types. Also, unsupported or ambiguous setup directives should be normalized early, because they can hide whether a design problem is strategic or merely structural.\n15. When a solitaire has no reliable \u201csink\u201d for completed structures, the state space can stay busy without ever getting closer to a win. Giving the player a clear build rule plus an explicit way to cash out finished sequences often improves both solvability and decision quality more than adding extra piles or looser movement alone.\n16. When a solitaire variant shows near-zero card and pile usage with most losses fully exhausted after only a few moves, the problem is usually structural illegality or overconstraint, not \u201cdifficulty.\u201d In that situation, first repair the move grammar so common cards can participate in legal moves before tuning challenge.\n17. If a pile type appears in the initial layout but has no legal moves attached to it, it often functions as pure dead weight and can make the game provably unwinnable. A good repair is usually to either give that pile an active role in the move network or exclude it from the victory burden, rather than just loosening unrelated rules.\n18. When every simulation wins with identical move counts and very low pile usage, check for a trivial win condition or a dead move rule before tuning difficulty; deterministic \u201cwins\u201d often mean the game is bypassing its intended interaction entirely.\n\nA good repair is to make the main piles necessary for victory and to add a simple but nontrivial move rule that creates choices among multiple exposed cards, rather than merely slowing the automatic path to the current win condition.\n19. When a solitaire loses every sampled run by hitting the search cap rather than exhausting quickly, the problem is often not just \u201ctoo hard\u201d but \u201ctoo many low-value legal moves.\u201d Tightening the core move logic into a clear progression toward the win can improve both solvability and decision quality more than simply adding extra freedom.\n20. When a solitaire variant shows only losses at the search cap, first check for rule combinations that generate lots of legal moves without meaningful progress. Tightening a noisy destination while making one core objective pile plainly reachable often improves both solvability and decision quality more than adding entirely new mechanics.\n21. When a solitaire generates only losses at the search cap, the problem is often not \u201ctoo hard\u201d so much as \u201ctoo many low-value legal moves.\u201d Tightening moves into a consistent building rule can improve both solvability and decision quality by reducing aimless state explosion.\n22. When a prototype is failing every run at the search cap, first check for malformed or nonfunctional rules before tuning difficulty; \u201cimpossible\u201d and \u201ctoo many meaningless moves\u201d can look similar in bot results. A good repair is often to give the game a clear sink for progress (like foundations or removable completed runs) so tableau moves serve an actual win condition instead of just rearrangement.\n23. When a loss pattern shows 0% wins with low pile usage and the win condition references piles that no move can affect, fix connectivity before tuning difficulty: every pile named in the objective should participate in at least one legal path toward progress.\n\nIf a design is malformed enough that whole subsystems are inert, a small \u201cdifficulty tweak\u201d won\u2019t help; first restore a valid rules loop where the source, tableau, and goal piles all interact.\n24. If the win condition targets a pile type, that pile type must have a clear path for cards to leave it; otherwise the game becomes unwinnable regardless of how many other legal moves exist. When a prototype shows near-zero card usage and some fully exhausted unwinnable runs, first check for disconnected mechanics before tuning difficulty.\n25. When a solitaire variant shows mostly long inconclusive losses rather than quick dead-ends, the problem is often not a total lack of mobility but a move network that generates many reversible states without enough productive consolidation. A good next step is to simplify each pile type\u2019s role and slightly broaden one core building rule, instead of adding more exotic exceptions.\n26. If the win condition requires tableau piles to become empty, the rules need at least one non-tableau destination that can permanently absorb cards; otherwise every move only rearranges cards and no win state can exist. When a variant proves unwinnable on every shuffle with zero card usage, first check for a missing \"sink\" for cards before tuning difficulty.\n27. When a solitaire fails on every shuffle with very short, fully exhausted searches, look first for structural impossibilities rather than \u201cdifficulty\u201d: unreachable pile types, impossible suit/rank tests, or win conditions that require clearing piles with no legal outgoing moves.\n\nA good first repair is to ensure every major pile type has a role in both play and victory; unused tableau piles and destinations that cannot legally accept cards usually signal dead design space.\n28. When every simulated deal has zero legal moves, first look for structural deadlocks before tuning difficulty: impossible condition clauses, too few destination piles, or rules that require a destination top card when none can ever be exposed. A small rules repair that guarantees initial mobility is usually more valuable than adding complexity.\n29. When most losses are fully exhausted after 0\u20131 moves, the problem is usually not \u201cdifficulty\u201d but missing move connectivity: the rule graph doesn\u2019t let the opening layout develop. A good fix is often to simplify one core movement rule and align the win condition with broad card clearance, so more of the layout must actually participate in a win.\n30. When a solitaire variant fails by hitting search limits on every run, the problem is often too many reversible or low-value legal moves rather than pure difficulty. Giving each pile type a clearer role and making the win condition match that role can reduce state-space churn while improving both solvability and decision quality.\n31. A solitaire can look \u201csuccessful\u201d in simulation while being broken if the win condition is satisfied by a trivial source-only action like emptying the draw pile. When wins are uniform, extremely short, and show negligible card/pile usage, first fix the objective so victory depends on interacting with the main tableau rather than on consuming a bookkeeping pile.\n32. When a solitaire variant stalls at the search cap on every deal, the problem is often not just difficulty but overly permissive moves that create many reversible, non-progress states. Tightening a broad stack-move rule so it encodes a clear building structure can both reduce state explosion and make the game more strategically legible.\n33. If a game wins as soon as the stock is exhausted, the stock often becomes the only meaningful resource and tableau play becomes incidental. Requiring the active piles to be cleared as part of victory is a simple way to force broader card usage and give the rest of the layout a genuine role.\n34. When a game is losing on every shuffle with full state-space exhaustion, first check for disconnected pile types and win-condition piles that have no legal way to change. A solitaire layout usually needs a clear card-flow graph from every populated pile either toward the win or toward a helper pile that in turn feeds the win.\n35. When a bot always times out rather than quickly exhausting, suspect overly broad reversible moves before assuming the game is merely \"hard.\" Tightening a permissive rule to require meaningful destination relationships often improves solvability more than adding new freedoms, because it turns search churn into progress.\n36. When a solitaire variant shows only deep timeouts and no wins, first check whether the move graph is structurally complete: players usually need a working tableau rule, a way to move from stock/tableau into a sink pile, and a win condition aligned with those sinks. Repairing missing roles is often more important than tuning difficulty.\n37. A solitaire design can fail completely from a few mismatched primitives: if move rules reference suits or destinations that the actual deck and pile graph can never satisfy, the result is not \u201chard\u201d but provably dead. Before tuning difficulty, first ensure every pile has a reachable purpose and that the win condition is compatible with the allowed card flow.\n38. When a solitaire generates many explored states but no wins, the problem is often not \u201ctoo hard\u201d so much as \u201cthe move rules don\u2019t create progress.\u201d Prefer move rules that define a clear constructive structure (for example, descending builds with stack-preservation requirements) over permissive but aimless movement.\n39. A solitaire variant that wins in 0 moves is usually suffering from a broken win condition, not from being \u201ceasy\u201d; fixing that should come before any balance tuning. Also, if a pile type appears in the layout but has no meaningful inbound or outbound moves, it often becomes either irrelevant clutter or an accidental loophole in the win logic.\n40. When every simulated deal is proven unwinnable almost immediately, fix reachability before tuning difficulty: make sure the start state is valid, the key pile type named in the win condition can actually lose cards, and at least one move rule meaningfully advances toward that win condition.\n41. When a solitaire variant shows very low card usage and repeated search-cap losses across all shuffles, the problem is often structural rather than merely \"too hard\": invalid setup syntax, unreachable cards, or moves that don't connect the game's piles into a coherent flow. In those cases, a small, legal ruleset with clear progression is more valuable than trying to tune isolated conditions inside a broken state space.\n42. When a solitaire variant shows 0 legal moves from every initial shuffle, the first fix should be to restore a basic \u201cmovement grammar\u201d the random deal can actually satisfy: usually a permissive single-card move plus a stack rule that shares the same ordering logic and allows empty-column recovery. Restrictive destination filters without a corresponding rank relationship often create state spaces with no edges at all.\n43. If a solitaire has no strong \u201csink\u201d that permanently advances cards toward victory, broad movement rules often create a large reversible state space instead of meaningful progress. Constraining at least one pile type into foundation-like buildup can both clarify objectives and reduce search degeneracy without removing all player choice.\n44. When simulations show extremely low card usage and occasional fully exhausted unwinnable states, the next step is usually not \u201cmake it easier\u201d in the abstract, but to repair the game\u2019s movement graph so cards can actually circulate between the main pile types. A solitaire layout becomes meaningful only when its central piles both accept incoming cards under common situations and contribute directly to the win condition.\n45. When a solitaire repeatedly fails after short, low-usage searches, the problem is often not \u201cdifficulty\u201d but disconnected rules: too few legal transitions between pile types. A good next step is to ensure every starting pile both sends cards somewhere meaningful and can be reached by at least one other pile type.\n46. A solitaire position is usually unwinnable if cards have no way to leave circulation but the win condition requires piles to empty. Before tuning difficulty, make sure the rules include at least one genuine \u201csink\u201d pile or removal mechanism that converts progress into a reachable win state.\n47. When a game shows very low card usage and every loss hits the search cap, the issue is often not \u201ctoo hard\u201d but \u201cthe legal move graph is malformed\u201d: either almost no productive moves exist, or a permissive rule creates lots of meaningless cycling. A good fix is usually to replace one vague or contradictory core rule with a single clear progression mechanic that gives many legal moves while still pointing toward a specific win condition.\n48. When a solitaire repeatedly proves unwinnable with very low card usage, first look for an opening-move bottleneck rather than adding more structure. A single over-gated move rule can make most shuffles dead on arrival; relaxing that gate is often a better first fix than rewriting the whole game.\n49. When a solitaire variant shows uniformly low card/pile usage and every bot run stalls at the search cap, first look for rule-level dead zones: impossible suit references, stack rules with no internal structure requirement, or win conditions that ignore whole pile types. Repairing unreachable or purposeless mechanics usually helps more than adding complexity.\n50. When every simulated deal has zero legal moves from the initial state, the next revision should prioritize restoring basic move connectivity before tuning difficulty. In solitaire, a few empty piles or a broad \u201ccan build on empty / standard build relation\u201d rule often serves as essential scaffolding that lets the rest of the design express meaningful decisions.\n51. When a solitaire has many initialized pile types but only one legal source/destination path, unwinnability often comes from \u201cdead inventory\u201d rather than difficulty. A good first repair is to align the move graph with the win condition so every pile that must be emptied actually has a way to shed cards.\n52. A solitaire can appear \"perfectly winnable\" for the wrong reason if its win condition tracks only source depletion rather than meaningful board progress. When every simulation wins in the same tiny move count with very low card/pile usage, first check whether the victory condition bypasses the main play space before tuning move difficulty.\n53. A solitaire can become trivial if its win condition tracks only depletion of a source pile rather than meaningful organization or clearance of the tableau. When wins happen with very low card and pile usage, strengthen the objective so that the core play area\u2014not just the draw mechanism\u2014must be engaged to finish the game.\n54. If a solitaire ruleset has no broadly usable tableau-building move, the game often becomes unwinnable regardless of shuffle; adding even a simple, consistent build rule usually matters more than tweaking the deal or win condition. Also, when a draw is gated by tableau state, make sure the tableau rules can actually create and resolve that state rather than deadlocking immediately.\n55. If all legal moves flow in only one direction into non-clearing piles, \"empty all piles\" style win conditions often become impossible even when many moves exist. A small set of reversible moves plus a win condition that matches where cards are meant to end up usually fixes dead-end designs more effectively than adding more starting complexity.\n56. If a solitaire variant loses with zero legal moves on most shuffles, first check accessibility before tuning difficulty: hidden starts and overly narrow first-move rules can make a design dead on arrival regardless of its deeper structure.\n\nA good early repair is to ensure most starting cards are actually reachable, then add one broad \u201ccirculation\u201d move rule so the bot and the player can create space; only after that is it meaningful to judge whether the win condition is too easy or too hard.\n57. When a solitaire fails with zero card and pile usage across every run, treat it first as a rules-connectivity problem, not a difficulty-tuning problem: make sure cards can actually move out of every starting area and that the win condition refers to reachable game states.\n\nA small, coherent repair that gives every pile a clear role is usually better than tweaking numeric constraints inside an otherwise disconnected ruleset.\n58. A solitaire win condition should measure completion of the actual play area, not just exhaustion of an input source like a draw pile; otherwise the game often collapses into repeated dealing with no meaningful decisions. When a design shows identical move counts and very low pile usage across all shuffles, first fix the objective before tuning difficulty.\n59. If a deal-style draw is the main way new cards enter the game, gating it behind a condition that's rarely achievable from the start can freeze the entire design. When a game stalls with very low card usage and every loss hits the search cap, first check whether the draw access rule is preventing the state space from opening up at all.\n60. A solitaire layout needs at least one legal opening move in a wide range of shuffles; if every move depends on an empty destination, the initial deal must include an empty pile or the game is instantly unwinnable. Small structural fixes to create move availability are often more important than adding complexity.\n61. A draw-gating rule should usually restrict draw at meaningful board states, not at the source itself; a condition like \u201cdraw only when DRAW is empty\u201d silently disables the core flow of the game. When a solitaire uses a large deal stock, it also needs a clear sink for finished structures; otherwise even generous movement rules can leave the board unwinnable because nothing ever truly leaves play.\n62. If a game loses every run with zero legal moves, fix move legality before tuning difficulty. A single contradictory condition can make an otherwise interesting layout completely dead, so restoring a basic move loop is the highest-leverage change.\n63. When losses are fully exhausted with near-zero usage, treat it as a rules-connectivity failure before a difficulty problem: first ensure the game has legal syntax, at least one meaningful path from the starting piles to the win piles, and that every major pile type participates in that path.\n\nA small repair that assigns clear roles to existing pile types is usually more informative than a total rewrite; once the game becomes actually playable, later rounds can tune difficulty and decision density.\n64. When a large draw pile exists but no move rule references `DRAW` as a source, most of the deck becomes dead content; fixing access to stranded cards is usually a higher priority than tuning difficulty. In solitaire iteration, first make sure every major component of the setup can participate in play, then refine the constraints on how it participates.\n65. A solitaire that can be won by exhausting the stock alone is usually missing a real bottleneck; tying victory to clearing active piles forces the tableau rules to matter. When a design shows identical move counts and low pile usage across all shuffles, adding more interactive destinations and a sequence-removal mechanism is often a better fix than merely lowering win rate.\n66. When a solitaire variant has many legal moves but no reliable way to remove cards from circulation, the result is often a large, wandering state space rather than meaningful challenge. A small set of coherent progression rules\u2014especially a true outlet such as foundations with a clear win condition\u2014usually improves playability more than adding yet more move freedom.\n67. A solitaire can look busy yet be fundamentally unwinnable if its legal moves don't interact with the piles named in the win condition. Before tuning difficulty, first ensure that every critical pile type both participates in moves and can plausibly move toward the win state.\n68. When a game loses immediately with full exhaustion and near-zero usage, fix structural reachability before tuning difficulty: make sure every pile type participates in legal moves, the draw can actually discharge cards, and the win condition is achievable from the available actions.\n\nSmall \u201crepair\u201d revisions are often better than chasing theme too early; a minimally coherent move network gives playtest data that reflects difficulty and decision quality instead of pure rules failure.\n69. When a solitaire variant shows 0% card and pile usage across exhaustive losses, treat it as a rules-definition failure before a balance problem: first restore a coherent state space with legal opening moves and a win condition tied to the actual play area.\n\nA compact repair is often better than a full thematic rewrite: preserve the core interaction you want to test, but rebuild the setup around valid pile definitions and obvious card flow so simulation can reveal gameplay rather than syntax-adjacent dead ends.\n70. When a ruleset references colors or suits that the deck does not contain, the game can become accidentally impossible or degenerate even if it still compiles. Before tuning difficulty, first make sure every move condition is actually satisfiable by the cards in play.\n71. A single over-restrictive gating rule can make an otherwise rich layout functionally dead; if a draw is central to progression, its availability rule should usually prevent only known failure states, not the draw action itself.\n\nWhen a solitaire variant is producing lots of explored states but zero wins, prefer fixing broken move logic with one coherent core structure over adding more exceptions; players need a clear route from local moves to actual clearance.\n72. When a solitaire has 0 legal moves on every deal, fix basic move reachability before tuning difficulty: ensure at least one common opening action exists, usually by allowing movement to empty spaces or by providing a low-threshold build rule.\n\nFor tiny-deck variants especially, every rule should match the deck\u2019s actual composition; suit/color conditions that reference missing suits can silently make the entire game unwinnable.\n73. When every tested deal has zero legal moves from the start, prioritize repairing setup validity and guaranteeing at least one broad, low-friction move pattern before tuning difficulty. A small solitaire needs a playable core loop first; only after cards can actually circulate does it make sense to tighten constraints for challenge.\n74. When a solitaire variant shows many \"proven unwinnable\" starts with near-zero card usage, the first fix is usually not balance but basic move connectivity: make sure most dealt cards have at least one plausible destination and that the win condition aligns with actually clearing the layout rather than just exhausting a source pile.\n75. If a solitaire win condition only tracks depletion of a stock or draw pile, automated play will often expose a degenerate \u201cclick to win\u201d loop. Tie victory to the actual play area being resolved, and make sure newly dealt cards have productive destinations so the rest of the layout participates in winning.\n76. If every legal move only adds cards to piles and nothing ever removes or clears them, an \u201call piles empty\u201d win condition becomes structurally impossible no matter how much draw access you provide. Solitaire rules need at least one genuine outlet that converts local progress into irreversible global progress.\n77. When a variant shows many proven unwinnable deals and even zero-move starts, first repair rule coherence before tuning difficulty: impossible suit/color requirements, dead move destinations, or unreachable win conditions overwhelm any more subtle balance changes. A small set of consistent movement rules plus a realistic win condition usually improves both solvability and decision density more than adding new mechanics.\n78. If a large share of the deck starts in a draw pile, that pile must both have a legal way to feed the tableau and be included in the win logic; otherwise the game can become provably unwinnable regardless of the tableau rules.\n\nWhen a variant is failing on every seed with fully exhausted searches and near-zero usage, first check for structural dead content (unreachable cards, impossible win condition, malformed draw setup) before tuning difficulty.\n79. When a solitaire variant fails with zero legal moves on nearly every deal, first check for rule contradictions and malformed setup before tuning difficulty; a single impossible condition can make every other design choice irrelevant.\n\nA good \u201crepair\u201d pass should restore legal starts and a coherent win condition with minimal new mechanics, so later iterations can tune challenge from a working baseline instead of debugging noise.\n80. When a solitaire design produces only long inconclusive searches, first check for malformed or contradictory rules before tuning difficulty: invalid setup syntax and overly strange move predicates can create huge state spaces without meaningful progress. A good repair step is to preserve the layout and card distribution while replacing exotic, low-signal move rules with a small coherent ruleset the bot can actually explore.\n81. A solitaire variant that wins while leaving most piles untouched usually has a mis-specified victory condition, not a well-balanced ruleset. Tightening the win condition should usually be paired with at least one flexible \u201cpositioning\u201d move, so the newly relevant piles become part of the play rather than dead weight.\n82. When a bot spends many moves exploring but rarely wins, the problem is often not just difficulty but an overly broad move graph that generates lots of low-value states. Adding simple structural constraints to high-volume move routes can improve both solvability and decision quality more than loosening the game further.\n\nA solitaire win condition should usually require clearing or organizing all of the play area that the player meaningfully interacts with; otherwise, low-usage \u201cwins\u201d can hide a degenerate objective.\n83. If most simulated deals have zero legal moves, fix reachability before tuning difficulty: a solitaire game needs at least one broadly available early action, or all later structure is irrelevant.\n\nA win condition should point at piles the player can actually change. If the winning piles are never legal sources or destinations, the game is usually unwinnable regardless of shuffle.\n84. If a solitaire layout starts with all cards trapped in tableau piles, it usually needs either a clear \u201csink\u201d pile (such as a foundation/discard) or strong tableau-building rules that can create space; otherwise the game may be provably unwinnable from most shuffles. When a bot reports many zero-move losses with full exhaustion, fixing the core state-flow of cards is more important than tuning difficulty.\n85. When simulations show 0% card and pile usage across all runs, treat that as a structural rules failure before tuning difficulty: the game likely has no meaningful legal opening moves or inaccessible starting material.\n\nA good next step is usually a minimal \u201cmake the core loop real\u201d repair: ensure the draw is valid, every starting pile type participates in moves or win conditions, and the main build/remove pattern is expressible before adding extra constraints.\n86. When a game loses by wandering for thousands of moves instead of getting stuck, the problem is often excessive permissiveness rather than excessive restriction. Tightening one or two high-branching move rules into recognizable build patterns can improve solvability and decision quality more than adding new mechanics.\n87. When a solitaire fails with zero legal moves on every deal, the right fix is usually structural, not numeric: add at least one broadly-available legal move and make the win condition reachable through that move. Also, ensure every condition actually matches the deck and initial layout; a single suit or face-state mismatch can silently collapse the entire game tree.\n88. When a solitaire variant shows 0% wins with every run hitting the search cap, the problem is often not \u201ctoo hard\u201d but \u201cill-posed\u201d: legal moves may be either too scarce to make progress or too unconstrained to create an enormous, unproductive search tree. A good repair is usually a focused rewrite toward one clear role per pile type and one coherent build logic for each.\n89. When a solitaire variant has 0 legal moves from the initial state, first check for rule/data mismatches before tuning difficulty: impossible suit tests, self-contradictory draw gates, and win conditions that can never be reached will overpower every other design choice.\n\nA good early repair is to restore a basic gameplay loop with one clear building direction and one clear sink pile; once the game becomes playable at all, you can iterate on difficulty and decision depth.\n90. When a solitaire wins on every shuffle with nearly identical move counts, look first for a move rule that effectively turns empty piles into unrestricted teleporters. Adding just enough placement structure to tableau-to-tableau moves often increases meaningful choices more effectively than adding more piles or more cards.\n91. When a game loses on every shuffle without exhausting the state space, first check for rule incoherence before tuning difficulty: impossible or near-impossible move predicates can make search balloon without creating real decisions. Also, a win condition tied only to exhausting the stock is usually too weak unless the stock\u2019s dealt cards can also be meaningfully cleared elsewhere.\n92. When a solitaire ruleset produces only search-cap losses with high pile usage, the problem is often not \u201ctoo hard\u201d but \u201ctoo unconstrained\u201d: permissive or structureless moves can explode the state space without creating progress. A small set of clear build rules and a concrete objective usually improves solvability more than adding more piles or cards.\n93. When a solitaire has no true sink for completed work, search tends to bloat without producing wins; adding a clear \u201ccompletion\u201d move often improves both solvability and decision quality more than loosening tableau rules alone.\n\nBefore tuning difficulty, first repair malformed or self-contradictory move rules into a small coherent core; otherwise playtest results mostly reflect parser/default behavior and dead rules rather than the intended design.\n94. If a pile type appears in the setup and win condition but has no outbound move, it often becomes dead weight or an unwinnable blocker. In solitaire design, every pile named in the objective should usually have a clear mechanical path for cards to leave it, and early revisions should prioritize restoring that basic flow before tuning difficulty.\n95. When a solitaire repeatedly hits the search cap without any wins, the problem is often not \u201ctoo hard\u201d in a strategic sense but \u201cinsufficiently connected\u201d move rules: the player can shuffle cards around, but not in ways that create progress toward the win condition. A good fix is usually to add one broadly useful transfer rule that links a stranded pile type back into the main flow of play, rather than rewriting every rule at once.\n96. If every legal move depends on suits or pile states that can never occur in the actual deck/setup, the game collapses into a proven zero-move loss. First fix \u201creachability\u201d bugs like impossible suit checks before tuning difficulty; a trivially unwinnable game gives no useful signal about deeper design quality.\n97. When a solitaire rule lets cards move into holding piles with almost no restriction and no meaningful way back out, it often creates a huge but unproductive search space rather than real decision-making. Tightening the destination rule into a clear long-term structure, while adding a limited undo lane, usually improves both solvability and strategic texture.\n98. When every simulated deal is proven unwinnable very quickly, check the win condition against the move graph before tuning difficulty: a pile that must end empty needs at least one plausible way for its starting cards to leave permanently. Small, targeted repairs to initial counts and one pile\u2019s role are often better than a full rewrite when diagnosing a structural impossibility.\n99. When a solitaire variant has 0 legal moves from the initial deal, the next revision should first establish a basic playable loop before tuning difficulty. A small set of conventional, internally consistent move rules usually teaches more than trying to salvage a fundamentally disconnected move economy.\n100. A solitaire can look \u201cactive\u201d in simulation while still being unwinnable if all legal moves merely reshuffle cards within a closed system. Make sure the rules include a genuine route from the starting area to a distinct win-progress area, and align the win condition with that route.\n\nProduce the next version of skill.md: rewrite it as a whole, integrating what's genuinely new or well-supported from the insights above into the existing document rather than appending to it. Keep the entire document to at most 20 bullet points total, organized under a small number of clear headers. End your response with the complete new skill.md in a single fenced code block tagged `skill`, as the last thing in your response."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "```skill\n# skill.md\n\n## Build a playable core first\n- Treat 0-move starts, near-zero card usage, and universal unwinnability as structural failures before difficulty problems. First restore a legal, coherent core loop; only then tune challenge.\n- Guarantee broad opening mobility across many shuffles. A solitaire needs at least one common early move, usually via a simple build rule, movement to empty spaces, or another low-friction transfer that the initial layout can actually satisfy.\n- Validate rule/setup compatibility early. Impossible suit/color tests, unsupported setup directives, contradictory predicates, or source/destination references the engine cannot realize can make a design functionally dead even if it looks close on paper.\n- Make the move graph connected. Every populated pile type should either send cards toward progress or feed a helper pile that does; piles named in the objective must be reachable and changeable through legal play.\n- If a large share of cards starts in stock/draw, that source must actually expose cards into play. Draw-gating rules should be used sparingly and should not block the game\u2019s main pacing mechanism at the source itself.\n\n## Align card flow with the win condition\n- A win condition should measure resolution of the actual play area, not mere depletion of a bookkeeping source like stock/draw. If wins happen with very low pile or card usage, the objective is probably bypassing the intended game.\n- If the win requires piles to empty, the rules need a genuine sink: foundations, removals, completed-run cash-outs, or another irreversible outlet. Rearrangement alone cannot satisfy an emptying objective.\n- Every major component should have a real role in victory. If a pile type appears in setup but has no meaningful moves, or can receive cards but not release them, it becomes dead weight, a pointless sink, or an accidental blocker.\n- Pile roles should be clear and consistent: source piles expose cards, tableau reorganizes them, and at least one destination type accumulates durable progress. Designs become more coherent when victory matches those roles.\n\n## Control state-space shape\n- Search-cap losses often mean the game is ill-posed, not just hard: either productive moves are missing, or there are too many reversible, low-value moves. Diagnose structure before adjusting numbers.\n- Prefer a small set of coherent progression rules over exotic or overly broad movement. Clear build relationships create strategy and solvability better than permissive moves that only increase branching.\n- When legal moves are plentiful but aimless, tighten the highest-volume routes so destinations encode meaningful structure. This usually improves both decision quality and bot solvability more than adding new freedoms.\n- When legal moves are too scarce, add one broadly useful primitive action rather than many special cases. A single circulation or consolidation move often repairs deadness more effectively than a large rewrite.\n- Avoid unrestricted holding areas. Piles that accept cards easily but do not advance victory or release cards cleanly tend to create large, unproductive search spaces.\n\n## Design for relevance, not loopholes\n- Tighten trivial objectives before adding mechanics. If the game wins in identical short lines or even at 0 moves, fix the win condition first so the main tableau and active piles must matter.\n- If some piles or cards remain mostly unused in successful play, reconnect them to the objective before increasing complexity. Requiring existing play areas to participate is usually stronger than adding new subsystems.\n- Make at least one destination type a long-term home for completed work. Without an explicit completion pattern, solitaire tends to produce churn instead of progress.\n- Early repair passes should be minimal and informative: preserve the intended interaction, but normalize the rules into a compact, internally consistent system that can reveal real difficulty and decision-making.\n```"}]